---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const title = "BSL Numbers 1 to 5: How to Count on Your Fingers";
const description =
  "Learn the BSL number signs 1–5 with clear handshape descriptions, how they differ from ASL numbers, and why 0 and 6–10 vary by region.";
const datePublished = "2026-10-14";
const slug = "bsl-numbers-1-to-5";
const pageUrl = `https://signlanguage-translator.com/learn/${slug}/`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Are BSL numbers one-handed or two-handed?",
    a: "One-handed. Like most BSL counting, numbers 1 to 5 are signed with the dominant hand alone. The two-handed alphabet is used for letters, not for these number signs.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do BSL numbers look like ASL numbers?",
    a: "Not quite. ASL numbers are also one-handed, but the handshapes and palm orientations differ — for example, BSL 1 points the index finger up with the palm facing inward, while ASL 1 holds the index finger with the palm facing the signer. Check the alphabet page, which shows both systems side by side.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why doesn't this guide cover 0 and 6–10?",
    a: "Because they vary by region. BSL number signs above 5 (and zero) differ between parts of the UK, so describing one version as 'the' BSL sign would be misleading. This site covers the stable, widely agreed forms: 1 to 5.",
  },
  {
    q: "Which hand do I count with?",
    a: "Your dominant hand — the hand you write with. Keep it up in front of your body where the other person can see it clearly.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I practise BSL numbers without looking like I'm guessing?",
    a: "Count real things: items on your desk, stairs as you climb them, minutes on a timer. Counting in context cements the handshapes far faster than drilling the numbers in sequence alone.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I learn BSL numbers from ASL resources?",
    a: "No — don't cross the streams. The systems look similar at a glance but differ in real details like palm orientation and finger selection. Learn BSL numbers from BSL sources and ASL numbers from ASL sources.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do you count to 10 in BSL?",
    a: "This site covers the stable, widely agreed signs: 1 to 5. The signs for 0 and 6–10 vary by region across the UK, so we don't describe a single version as 'the' BSL sign. Learn higher numbers from a Deaf instructor or a regional BSL resource.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do you sign your age in BSL?",
    a: "Honestly: signing an age needs the number signs for the specific number, and since this site covers BSL 1–5 only, ages above 5 aren't covered here. Learn the full 1–31 range from a Deaf instructor or a regional BSL resource before signing ages.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do you sign phone numbers in BSL?",
    a: "Phone numbers need digit signs beyond 5, which vary by region — and phone-digit conventions aren't something to guess at from text descriptions. This site doesn't cover them; a Deaf instructor or regional BSL course is the honest route.",
  },
];

const articleJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: title,
  description,
  datePublished,
  author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Firoz Khan" },
  publisher: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "SignLanguage-Translator.com",
  },
  mainEntityOfPage: pageUrl,
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout title={`${title} | Sign Language Translator`} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleJsonLd)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqJsonLd)} />

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-14 sm:px-6">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-sm text-slate-500">
        <li><a class="underline-offset-2 hover:underline" href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
        <li><a class="underline-offset-2 hover:underline" href="/learn/">Learn</a></li>
        <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
        <li aria-current="page" class="font-semibold text-slate-900">{title}</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="mt-6 text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-500">
      <time datetime={datePublished}>{datePublished}</time> · By Firoz Khan · 5 min read
    </p>

    <article class="prose-lg mt-6 space-y-5 text-slate-700">
      <p>
        Counting is one of the first practical skills in any sign language —
        and BSL numbers 1 to 5 are the perfect place to start, because they
        are the most stable, widely agreed number signs in BSL. Each is
        one-handed and signed with the dominant hand. Here they are, one number
        at a time.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">1 — index finger up</h2>
      <div class="flex justify-center" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="text-7xl font-extrabold text-indigo-700">1</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        With your dominant hand, raise just the index finger and fold the
        remaining fingers into a fist, with your palm facing inward toward
        you. Think of it as pointing straight up.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">2 — two fingers in a V</h2>
      <div class="flex justify-center" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="text-7xl font-extrabold text-indigo-700">2</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        Raise your index and middle fingers together in a V shape, keeping the
        rest folded down. If you've seen the ASL 2, note the difference in
        palm orientation when you compare the two systems.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">3 — three fingers up</h2>
      <div class="flex justify-center" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="text-7xl font-extrabold text-indigo-700">3</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        Raise your index, middle, and ring fingers, folding the thumb and
        pinky down. Keep the three raised fingers straight and slightly apart
        so the number reads clearly at a glance.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">4 — four fingers, thumb tucked</h2>
      <div class="flex justify-center" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="text-7xl font-extrabold text-indigo-700">4</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        Point all four fingers upward and hold the thumb down against your
        palm. The thumb is the distinguishing feature here — tucked in means 4,
        out and extended with the rest means 5.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">5 — the full hand</h2>
      <div class="flex justify-center" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="text-7xl font-extrabold text-indigo-700">5</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        Extend all five fingers and hold them up in front of your body, spread
        naturally. This is the most distinctive number in the set — nothing
        else in BSL counting uses the full open hand this way.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">How BSL numbers differ from ASL numbers</h2>
      <p>
        ASL numbers 1–5 are also one-handed, which is why beginners confuse the
        two systems. But the details differ — palm orientation, which fingers
        are used for which numbers, and thumb placement all vary. Our{" "}
        <a class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline" href="/alphabet/">alphabet page</a>{" "}
        shows the ASL and BSL systems side by side, so you can compare directly
        instead of mixing them up.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-6 text-center">
        <div class="flex items-end justify-center gap-3">
          <img class="h-32 w-auto" loading="lazy" src="/numbers/1.svg" alt="ASL number handshape for 1" />
          <img class="h-32 w-auto" loading="lazy" src="/numbers/2.svg" alt="ASL number handshape for 2" />
          <img class="h-32 w-auto" loading="lazy" src="/numbers/3.svg" alt="ASL number handshape for 3" />
          <img class="h-32 w-auto" loading="lazy" src="/numbers/4.svg" alt="ASL number handshape for 4" />
          <img class="h-32 w-auto" loading="lazy" src="/numbers/5.svg" alt="ASL number handshape for 5" />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500">
          ASL number handshapes 1–5 (one-handed, palm facing the signer) — compare with the BSL forms above and spot the differences.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">An honest note on 0 and 6–10</h2>
      <p>
        This guide deliberately stops at 5. BSL number signs for 0 and 6–10
        vary by region across the UK, and describing one regional version as
        "the" BSL sign would be misleading. This site covers the stable,
        widely agreed forms — numbers 1 to 5 — and doesn't guess at the rest.
        When you're ready for higher numbers, learn them from a Deaf instructor
        or a regional BSL resource.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Counting practice tips</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
        <li>Count real things — items on your desk, stairs as you climb, minutes on a timer — rather than drilling 1–5 in sequence alone.</li>
        <li>Keep your hand up in front of your body where a conversation partner could see it; dropping it to waist level is hard to read.</li>
        <li>Practise the thumb transition between 4 (thumb tucked) and 5 (thumb out) until it's clean — that's the trickiest boundary in the set.</li>
        <li>Say the number aloud as you sign it at first; drop the voice once the handshapes are automatic.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <dl class="space-y-4">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
            <dt class="font-bold text-slate-900">{f.q}</dt>
            <dd class="mt-2 text-slate-700">{f.a}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Keep learning</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
        <li>
          <a class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline" href="/learn/asl-vs-bsl-key-differences/">ASL vs BSL: key differences</a>{" "}
          — why the two systems are completely separate languages.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline" href="/learn/how-to-fingerspell-your-name-in-bsl/">How to fingerspell your name in BSL</a>{" "}
          — the two-handed BSL alphabet, numbers' close companion.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline" href="/alphabet/">ASL &amp; BSL alphabet reference</a>{" "}
          — compare both systems side by side.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <div
      role="note"
      class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 px-5 py-4 text-sm text-slate-700"
    >
      <p>
        A learning aid, not a certified interpreter. Number signs are a
        starting point — real fluency comes from learning with Deaf instructors
        and the Deaf community.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
